WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.918 --> 00:00:14.861
So now I'd like to present our
speaker today, Professor Bill Burnett.

00:00:14.861 --> 00:00:18.537
So Bill is an adjunct professor
of mechanical engineering, and

00:00:18.537 --> 00:00:21.952
the Executive Director of
the Design Program at Stanford.

00:00:21.952 --> 00:00:25.342
He directs the undergraduate and
graduate program in the Design and

00:00:25.342 --> 00:00:27.220
teaches at the D.school.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.340
He received his bachelor's of science and

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.180
masters of science in
product design at Stanford.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.990
And has worked in start ups and
Fortune 500 companies,

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:38.331
including seven years at Apple,
designing award winning laptops.

00:00:38.331 --> 00:00:42.160
And a number of years in the toy industry,
designing Star Wars action figures.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.655
He holds a number of mechanical and
design patents and design awards.

00:00:46.655 --> 00:00:50.376
And in addition to his duties at Stanford,
he's on the Board of

00:00:50.376 --> 00:00:55.660
Socially Responsible Fashion startup, and
advises several other startup companies.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.080
So with that, Bill,
I'd like to turn it over to you.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:00.854
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:01:00.854 --> 00:01:04.093
Let's just get started here.

00:01:04.093 --> 00:01:07.112
And we try to create a little
bit of a provocative title,

00:01:07.112 --> 00:01:11.493
Need-Finding and Empathy: Why Your
Customers Can't Tell You What They Want.

00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:15.948
And from the looks of the number
of participants we have here,

00:01:15.948 --> 00:01:17.788
I guess the title works.

00:01:17.788 --> 00:01:21.242
But it's really an interesting
conversation, and I have this

00:01:21.242 --> 00:01:25.870
conversation a lot with people when they
either are reading about Design Thinking,

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.476
or they've maybe been to
one of our workshops,

00:01:28.476 --> 00:01:32.861
the Master Class which is coming up in
June, or one of the other workshops we do.

00:01:32.861 --> 00:01:36.540
And they go wait a minute
this Design Thinking thing,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.213
it's kind of pretty simple, isn't it?

00:01:39.213 --> 00:01:42.405
You just go out and
ask customers what they want.

00:01:42.405 --> 00:01:43.603
And then you build it.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:44.973
And then they say that's it.

00:01:44.973 --> 00:01:46.837
And then you're done, right?

00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:49.320
And it really doesn't work that way.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.218
So take a little bit of a step back
to just make sure everybody's on

00:01:54.218 --> 00:01:55.100
the same page.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.497
Design Thinking is both a process,
and what we called a set of mindsets,

00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:03.842
a way of thinking, or a culture that
you create in your organizations.

00:02:03.842 --> 00:02:07.807
And is this the pretty common diagram,
you see the following up of the D School

00:02:07.807 --> 00:02:10.555
website, and our website,
and the design program.

00:02:10.555 --> 00:02:15.793
[COUGH] It's a kind of a five step
process, you start with empathy,

00:02:15.793 --> 00:02:19.770
then you define or redefine the problem.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.975
Ideate just means have lots and lots of
ideas and then you prototype and test.

00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:27.170
Now this thing about this diagram
that is a little bit misleading,

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:31.353
is that it looks like it's in the process,
but in fact, it's not that at all.

00:02:31.353 --> 00:02:37.953
What happens is design teams enter
this process at various points.

00:02:37.953 --> 00:02:41.695
Maybe they have an idea already that
they want to try with customers,

00:02:41.695 --> 00:02:45.050
maybe they don't even know who
their customers are going to be if

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.610
they're a brand new start up.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.162
But this is sort of
the way we describe it.

00:02:49.162 --> 00:02:54.210
And the way I've always talked about these
kind of process diagrams is remember,

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.270
the diagram isn't the process.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:01.087
It's just a way of breaking it down so
we can teach it.

00:03:01.087 --> 00:03:03.090
I like to use an analogy to golf.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.252
If I were going to try to teach you
how to, try to learn to play golf,

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:08.902
I couldn't teach anyone
because I'm a terrible golfer.

00:03:08.902 --> 00:03:12.245
But I was going to learn to play golf,
the teacher would probably break

00:03:12.245 --> 00:03:15.780
the golf swing down into a number of
things, like how do I hold the club?

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:16.804
Whats the proper grip?

00:03:16.804 --> 00:03:20.739
How do you address the ball?
How do you place the ball on the tee?

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:22.027
How do you bring the club back?

00:03:22.027 --> 00:03:25.243
So each of these pieces would be
described as a separate step, but

00:03:25.243 --> 00:03:27.357
of course that's not what a golf swing is.

00:03:27.357 --> 00:03:31.800
A golf swing is a continuous,
a beautiful continuous motion which

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.385
results in the golf ball going
where you want it to go.

00:03:35.385 --> 00:03:38.174
So remember that the process diagram is

00:03:38.174 --> 00:03:42.406
the not the way the process
actually unfolds when you do it.

00:03:42.406 --> 00:03:46.760
That's one of the reasons we like to talk
about the mindsets of Design Thinking.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:52.831
[COUGH] The way in which designers think,
and the way in which if you have a culture

00:03:52.831 --> 00:03:57.831
of creativity, your design team,
or your company would think.

00:03:57.831 --> 00:03:59.719
I would say you start with curiosity.

00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:05.950
We're trained as engineers, designers,
people, if you're in marketing,

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.972
finance, whatever the discipline
you are in an organization,

00:04:10.972 --> 00:04:17.210
you're trained to think about the world
sort of with a skeptical rationalism.

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:21.880
You break problems down into smaller and
smaller steps.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:27.070
You rationalize them, you use your logic
and deductive reasoning to make decisions.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.988
That's not a particular useful way
to think about doing innovation.

00:04:31.988 --> 00:04:35.796
Because, when you're doing innovation,
you're moving into a future where nothing

00:04:35.796 --> 00:04:39.408
exists yet, or the things that you're
trying to invent haven't been invented.

00:04:39.408 --> 00:04:44.560
And so there really isn't much to
be skeptical or rational about.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.710
It's much more interesting and
much more generative to be curious.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.198
So, we start with curiosity,
and we typically,

00:04:51.198 --> 00:04:55.052
reframe the problem, that block called
define, I always call redefining,

00:04:55.052 --> 00:04:58.336
because typically we've been
given the wrong problem to solve.

00:04:58.336 --> 00:05:01.653
All of problems are sort of
out there in the world, but

00:05:01.653 --> 00:05:06.621
the real problem is something kind of
either deeper or at a more abstract level.

00:05:06.621 --> 00:05:09.218
We like radical collaboration
across multiple teams,

00:05:09.218 --> 00:05:12.532
we like to be mindful of our process,
because when you're in the middle

00:05:12.532 --> 00:05:16.399
of a converging part of the process where
you're trying to bring things together,

00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:19.569
it's important to know that's not
a good time for brainstorming.

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:22.718
When you're in a divergent part of
the process, where you're looking for lots

00:05:22.718 --> 00:05:26.860
more ideas, that's when you want to reach
out, collaborate, and brainstorm more.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.480
And then of course we have a bias to
action, because of these set of problems

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.571
that we're going to talk about next, about
why people can't tell you what they want.

00:05:34.571 --> 00:05:37.950
Since there's not a lot of
data to be analyzed, and

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.680
there's not much that we can plan with,
we just like to start by doing.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:49.982
So the kind of sort of a background,
I'll always say with Design Thinking,

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:54.083
we always said we start with
the people not the problem.

00:05:54.083 --> 00:05:56.810
I've been a consultant for 25, 30 years.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:02.634
Someone might come in and say hey, we're
a company and make this certain products,

00:06:02.634 --> 00:06:06.653
and we'd like to make in a new more
innovative versions of these products.

00:06:06.653 --> 00:06:10.913
And that's typically
the wrong place to start.

00:06:10.913 --> 00:06:13.299
We go back out and say,
well that's interesting,

00:06:13.299 --> 00:06:17.139
let's just start with who your users are,
who your customers are, and go there.

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:21.390
Because most problems are actually
solutions, masquerading as a problem.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:24.470
The New Wheelbarrow Project
is the classic example.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.920
I was doing a project for
a company that makes wheelbarrows.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.690
Actually, it's a slightly disguised
project, they made another really,

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.170
really common industrial product
that you'll find at every job site,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.557
but we're going to say
they made wheelbarrows.

00:06:36.557 --> 00:06:40.740
And they wanted us to do
innovative wheelbarrow.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.981
And so the problem, of course, with that
framing of the problem is that the problem

00:06:43.981 --> 00:06:45.436
already is described as a solution.

00:06:45.436 --> 00:06:49.598
They want another wheelbarrow,
they just want an innovative one.

00:06:49.598 --> 00:06:54.522
That might result in some
small product modifications.

00:06:54.522 --> 00:06:59.032
Two wheels instead of one,
better handles, more ergonomic,

00:06:59.032 --> 00:07:04.064
design of the lifting process so
that people don't hurt themselves.

00:07:04.064 --> 00:07:05.211
But at the end of the day,

00:07:05.211 --> 00:07:08.295
you're still going to end up with
a slightly modified wheelbarrow.

00:07:08.295 --> 00:07:08.813
And so,

00:07:08.813 --> 00:07:13.480
that's an example where the solution
is not actually what the problem is.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.776
The problem that a wheelbarrow solves,

00:07:15.776 --> 00:07:19.892
if you step back a second is that
there's workers all over a job site, and

00:07:19.892 --> 00:07:23.896
materials typically delivered to
the job site around the periphery.

00:07:23.896 --> 00:07:28.249
Because it's easier for the trucks to drop
things off in pallets full of, let's say,

00:07:28.249 --> 00:07:30.408
bricks, at the periphery of the job site.

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:35.131
And, therefore you can't get the brick
layers that are over somewhere else.

00:07:35.131 --> 00:07:38.835
And so, they come with their wheelbarrows,
they unload a few bricks into

00:07:38.835 --> 00:07:42.258
a wheelbarrow, and they walk over
to where they are doing the work.

00:07:42.258 --> 00:07:47.173
So the problem isn't wheelbarrows, the
problem is this location of workers and

00:07:47.173 --> 00:07:47.904
supplies.

00:07:47.904 --> 00:07:50.942
Once you frame the problem that way,
there's lots and

00:07:50.942 --> 00:07:52.592
lots of ways you can solve it,

00:07:52.592 --> 00:07:56.906
that are much more innovative than
simply thinking about a new wheelbarrow.

00:07:56.906 --> 00:07:59.620
I'll give you one example you could.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.030
Instead of delivering all the bricks
on pallets to edge of the job site,

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:08.200
you could have Amazon deliver the bricks
in individual small quantities with

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.090
their army of drones.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:14.060
In which case locating
each of the bricklayers

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:19.250
by the cell phone coordinates
in their pocket, their GPS

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.342
receivers in their cell phones would
simply transmit their location on site and

00:08:23.342 --> 00:08:27.230
the drone would put the bricks
exactly where they want them.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:28.920
You would never come to
a solution like that.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.610
And I'm not necessarily saying
that's a good solution.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:36.147
But you'd never come to a solution that
had that much innovation in it if you were

00:08:36.147 --> 00:08:38.259
just brainstorming wheelbarrows.

00:08:38.259 --> 00:08:43.015
So this whole notion that you just take
a group of users and ask them what they

00:08:43.015 --> 00:08:47.861
want, what they need, it just doesn't
work, and here's the reason why.

00:08:47.861 --> 00:08:50.573
This is a famous Henry Ford
quote that he said,

00:08:50.573 --> 00:08:55.044
if I'd asked people what they wanted,
they would have said faster horses.

00:08:55.044 --> 00:08:56.865
Now, just as a disclaimer here,

00:08:56.865 --> 00:09:01.171
I want to point out that It's most likely
that Henry Ford never never said that.

00:09:01.171 --> 00:09:03.163
We can't find any
historical evidence of it.

00:09:03.163 --> 00:09:06.812
But it's sort of a snarky statement,
it's something Henry might have said.

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:09.370
But his point was simply that
users don't know what they want,

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:11.450
they only know what they've got.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.871
And so
when you ask them about what they need,

00:09:13.871 --> 00:09:17.584
they give you variations of the things
that they already know about.

00:09:17.584 --> 00:09:20.777
And that's the first part of the problem.

00:09:20.777 --> 00:09:24.787
The problem with this need finding thing
is, you go out, and let's say you're,

00:09:24.787 --> 00:09:28.466
I was at Apple for seven years, but
long before they started doing phones.

00:09:28.466 --> 00:09:30.973
But I know some people from
the original phone team.

00:09:30.973 --> 00:09:32.512
But they knew they were
going to do a phone,

00:09:32.512 --> 00:09:34.293
that wasn't what the need
finding was about.

00:09:34.293 --> 00:09:38.223
The need finding was all about what
kind of a phone would the features be,

00:09:38.223 --> 00:09:42.749
how would data be presented, what would be
the sort of model of, the new paradigm.

00:09:42.749 --> 00:09:47.080
So let's say you're on a team like that,
and you've done a bunch of market research

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:51.413
and bunch of and you've determined that
users have a specific set of needs.

00:09:51.413 --> 00:09:54.380
You know,
needs to find the phone numbers usually,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.170
needs to be able to navigate to
website easily, things like that.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:02.797
So we've built this really high resolution
prototype which most people call a beta,

00:10:02.797 --> 00:10:05.176
the beta prototype that
objectively you and

00:10:05.176 --> 00:10:09.238
your team knows you've met all the needs
that the users have talked about.

00:10:09.238 --> 00:10:12.332
You show it to your customer
expecting that they will love it, and

00:10:12.332 --> 00:10:15.557
what they say is, the thing that
all engineers and designers hate.

00:10:15.557 --> 00:10:20.046
They say, yeah,
that's what I said I wanted.

00:10:20.046 --> 00:10:23.908
But now that I see that,
I've changed my mind.

00:10:23.908 --> 00:10:26.139
You know what I really want.

00:10:26.139 --> 00:10:28.284
And isn't that maddening?

00:10:28.284 --> 00:10:28.923
Right.

00:10:28.923 --> 00:10:31.892
And this is where the engineers go back
to the marketing people and say, hey,

00:10:31.892 --> 00:10:33.044
you didn't spec this right.

00:10:33.044 --> 00:10:34.890
Because we showed it to users, and

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.576
they had other needs that
you never told us about.

00:10:37.576 --> 00:10:41.680
And the marketing guys say well, we told
you to build exactly what they wanted.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.488
So if they didn't like it,
you must not have built what they wanted.

00:10:45.488 --> 00:10:49.891
But instead of getting into that
conversation, you just gotta

00:10:49.891 --> 00:10:55.137
acknowledge that humans are what is
called technically a wicked problem.

00:10:55.137 --> 00:11:00.952
Human beings do not stand still inside
some sort of market research bubble and

00:11:00.952 --> 00:11:06.246
their needs and their wants are not
consistent from moment to moment.

00:11:06.246 --> 00:11:10.669
And particularly when you've shown them
something that they never could have

00:11:10.669 --> 00:11:11.345
imagined.

00:11:11.345 --> 00:11:13.130
And here's the problem.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:17.036
Turns out a wicked problem, a wicked
problem is something that came out of

00:11:17.036 --> 00:11:20.063
social science, and
I think originally out of Berkley.

00:11:20.063 --> 00:11:24.456
Some guys who were studying urban planning
and why even though when you've built

00:11:24.456 --> 00:11:27.701
something everybody wants,
they don't like it any more.

00:11:27.701 --> 00:11:30.718
They said, a wicked problem is
a problem that's difficult or

00:11:30.718 --> 00:11:35.257
impossible to solve because of incomplete,
contradictory, and changing requirements.

00:11:35.257 --> 00:11:38.804
In addition to that, these requirements
are often difficult to recognize.

00:11:38.804 --> 00:11:43.657
So it's difficult to understand if you
really understood the requirements

00:11:43.657 --> 00:11:46.323
of users, because users are so complex.

00:11:46.323 --> 00:11:48.129
And here's, this is why.

00:11:48.129 --> 00:11:53.789
The data that you're looking for, the data
that you want, is about some future state.

00:11:53.789 --> 00:11:57.622
But that future state changes as
soon as you compose a solution.

00:11:57.622 --> 00:12:01.153
So I want a phone that makes it easy for
me to dial my phone numbers.

00:12:01.153 --> 00:12:03.730
You show me a phone with
a graphical user interface.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:04.984
And then I say, well,

00:12:04.984 --> 00:12:08.564
wow, if I had a graphical user interface,
I want a browser too.

00:12:08.564 --> 00:12:10.740
And that was never in the spec.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:17.909
But if you've shown me something that
changes my perception of what's possible.

00:12:17.909 --> 00:12:19.145
because I, as a user,

00:12:19.145 --> 00:12:23.931
only believe [INAUDIBLE] you could
maybe find a better way to do menus to

00:12:23.931 --> 00:12:28.406
solve my problem of finding my
phone numbers in my address book.

00:12:28.406 --> 00:12:30.566
But then later you show
me this other thing, and

00:12:30.566 --> 00:12:34.618
I realize, now if that's possible, then
all these other things are possible, and

00:12:34.618 --> 00:12:36.040
now I've changed what I want.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.240
And the users don't often, this notion
that requirements are difficult to

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.964
recognize, the users don't often recognize
their own needs, what we call latent or

00:12:44.964 --> 00:12:48.768
non-obvious needs, because of what
we call the expert problem, or

00:12:48.768 --> 00:12:51.547
what social scientists
call the expert problem.

00:12:51.547 --> 00:12:56.102
When you give a person a piece of
technology, if you remember the first time

00:12:56.102 --> 00:12:59.736
you used a spreadsheet or
the first time you used a browser.

00:12:59.736 --> 00:13:03.470
And maybe the user interface was clumsier,
difficult,

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:06.514
on the very first piece
of software you tried.

00:13:06.514 --> 00:13:09.709
You knew it was valuable to learn this,
so you worked really hard to learn it.

00:13:09.709 --> 00:13:13.664
And you figured out all these little
tricks, if you remember Lotus 1-2-3 or

00:13:13.664 --> 00:13:17.174
the early days of Excel, when it was
hard to figure out how to do things.

00:13:17.174 --> 00:13:21.111
So you figured out all these little tricks
that you retained, to quote, as expert.

00:13:21.111 --> 00:13:26.437
You were the person in the office who knew
how to use that piece of hard software.

00:13:26.437 --> 00:13:30.055
And so even though there were things in
the software that were difficult to use,

00:13:30.055 --> 00:13:33.889
and this applies to physical products or
any other kind of product where, you know,

00:13:33.889 --> 00:13:36.272
there's a little bit of
difficulties to overcome.

00:13:36.272 --> 00:13:40.695
And you become the expert, you've overcome
the difficulty, you know the workarounds.

00:13:40.695 --> 00:13:42.707
Then when I ask you, what do you need?

00:13:42.707 --> 00:13:45.900
You say, nothing, this works really well.

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:50.976
And I have to observe how you're using it
in order to understand all the ways in

00:13:50.976 --> 00:13:55.897
which you worked around difficulties
that are not obvious to you anymore,

00:13:55.897 --> 00:13:57.949
because you become an expert.

00:13:57.949 --> 00:14:03.207
Dave Liddle who was one of the founders of
Interval Research here in Silicon Valley,

00:14:03.207 --> 00:14:06.773
talked about this problem in
a couple of papers he wrote.

00:14:06.773 --> 00:14:13.073
And he was also at the early Xerox PARC
work on the Star microcomputers,

00:14:13.073 --> 00:14:17.168
which became
the Apple Macintosh interface.

00:14:17.168 --> 00:14:20.685
He talked about how software goes
through sort of three stages.

00:14:20.685 --> 00:14:22.997
First, only experts and gurus can use it.

00:14:22.997 --> 00:14:27.159
This is, you know, IBM mainframes
in an air conditioned room and

00:14:27.159 --> 00:14:29.060
technicians in white coats.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:34.277
And then it becomes technical software
that's used by a large group of people.

00:14:34.277 --> 00:14:38.613
But the technical nature of the software
requires developing expertise.

00:14:38.613 --> 00:14:41.594
And then the experts
hoard that knowledge and

00:14:41.594 --> 00:14:46.470
actually can't tell you about
the workarounds that they've invented.

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:51.650
Finally, software becomes easy
to use in consumer software.

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:56.689
So think of video games,
think of Nintendo, and things like that.

00:14:56.689 --> 00:15:01.317
And now all of the websites that you use
whose user interfaces are simple and

00:15:01.317 --> 00:15:05.448
easy, with a flick of a finger or
a thumb to navigate through them.

00:15:05.448 --> 00:15:08.904
And so now you're not expecting
to have expert knowledge,

00:15:08.904 --> 00:15:13.409
you're just expecting to be able to
use the product, because it's so easy.

00:15:13.409 --> 00:15:15.632
But we've entered the era of expectations,

00:15:15.632 --> 00:15:18.801
where everyone believes that
the product should be easy to use.

00:15:18.801 --> 00:15:23.488
And yet, the design of products is still
mired in this old idea of trying to

00:15:23.488 --> 00:15:28.490
capture the user requirements, writing
a document and then executing on it.

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:33.399
When in fact, pedagogically,
you cannot discover the true

00:15:33.399 --> 00:15:38.800
nature of the user's need by
simply interviewing and observing.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:43.352
You have to do the rest of the process,
which is to provoke the user's

00:15:43.352 --> 00:15:47.747
experience with a prototype,
super low resolution if possible,

