the abstract future,
or the concrete future.

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:22.190
And you're basically moving around
that process in the frameworks that

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:27.630
you're deriving concrete here is
your observations from the field,

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:32.890
observations, notes, videos,
interview transcripts.

00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:35.220
You move up into the abstract and

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:39.035
you start putting those Frameworks in
observations into some frameworks.

00:30:39.035 --> 00:30:42.167
It's first thing we always
talk about is the empathy map.

00:30:42.167 --> 00:30:44.802
The fact that people say and
do things in the world, but

00:30:44.802 --> 00:30:47.915
what you're really trying to get
to is what they think and feel.

00:30:47.915 --> 00:30:50.981
So you make a little two by two of this,
so you can feel,

00:30:50.981 --> 00:30:55.655
and you translate things from the same new
category to the think and feel category,

00:30:55.655 --> 00:30:58.430
using your own personal insight and
intuition.

00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:02.731
Knowing that you may be wrong,
this is where your points of view is not

00:31:02.731 --> 00:31:05.730
a hypothesis that's testable,
it's simply a point of view.

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:08.833
It's a user plus a need plus an insight,
and I think this is what they were,

00:31:08.833 --> 00:31:11.271
when they said this I think
this is what they were feeling.

00:31:11.271 --> 00:31:16.124
Let's go try to say if that's true by
building a prototype that would work

00:31:16.124 --> 00:31:18.720
if that thought were in fact accurate.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:24.900
Powerful but simple framework the
hierarchy of needs, this is Maslow's idea.

00:31:24.900 --> 00:31:29.318
There's a lot of work now that this
isn't necessarily a hierarchy anymore,

00:31:29.318 --> 00:31:34.070
that people cannot have
all of their safety and

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:38.510
physiological needs met and yet still be
working on self actualization even in

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:43.210
the poorest communities,
even in South Sudan in refugee camps.

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:47.700
Or people don't have security and
maybe even food and safety.

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:48.970
People still make jewelry.

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:53.440
People still write and sing songs.

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:58.030
They still perform artistic activities
that are sort of very human.

00:31:58.030 --> 00:32:02.900
So although it's not a hierarchy,
it's a good way of necessarily.

00:32:02.900 --> 00:32:04.280
Looking at things.

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:10.290
If I've made observations in
the safety and psychology area,

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:14.461
but I have no observations in the love,
belonging, esteem, and self-actualization,

00:32:14.461 --> 00:32:18.560
it gives me a to go back to the field
to ask different questions.

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:22.913
Or if I've found three of
the five things already, and

00:32:22.913 --> 00:32:28.118
I'm going to try to intuit the other two,
it gives me a framework for

00:32:28.118 --> 00:32:34.012
what's missing, and it drives the design
team to make better insights.

00:32:34.012 --> 00:32:37.755
And then finally, this one that comes out
of Dev Patnaik's book on need finding.

00:32:37.755 --> 00:32:40.950
Dev is the founder of Jump Associates.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:44.107
And also has written
a bunch on need finding.

00:32:44.107 --> 00:32:49.461
There's another book called Wired
to Care which is a business book.

00:32:49.461 --> 00:32:54.675
And he also teaches at Stanford
undergraduate need finding class.

00:32:54.675 --> 00:32:55.873
He's a great guy.

00:32:55.873 --> 00:33:00.258
Dev developed what they call
the simple mnemonic AEIOU syystem for

00:33:00.258 --> 00:33:02.300
breaking down observations.

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:04.884
A lot of times students come back in the
field and they got these videos and stuff,

00:33:04.884 --> 00:33:06.300
but they don't really know what they mean.

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:11.560
And it's a great way for kind of looking
at a video, encoding certain actions, and

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:13.440
then breaking those actions
down into insights.

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:18.706
Or the actual activities that
you observed, the team have a,

00:33:18.706 --> 00:33:23.180
the people you were looking at have a role
to play, were they just participants,

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:26.483
how is the power of the team
divided up around activities?

00:33:26.483 --> 00:33:27.969
What did you notice?

00:33:27.969 --> 00:33:31.328
Environments, where were they
doing what they were doing?

00:33:31.328 --> 00:33:33.860
Were they in a public space or
a private space?

00:33:33.860 --> 00:33:36.320
Was the place constrained or open?

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:38.110
Were they able to move things around or
not?

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:40.250
Interactions.

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:47.792
Were they interactions with people or
machines?

00:33:47.792 --> 00:33:49.884
And were the, what was the friction
in those interactions?

00:33:49.884 --> 00:33:51.860
How did the interactions occur?

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:54.115
What objects were they interacting with?

00:33:54.115 --> 00:33:58.390
Because if you can think about [INAUDIBLE]
how objects tell us what to do.

00:33:58.390 --> 00:34:01.312
A hammer's got a handle and
a head and you know exactly which.

00:34:01.312 --> 00:34:05.360
No one ever picks it up with the head and
tries to bang with the handle.

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.150
There's an old expression,
the hammer shapes the hand.

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:12.810
But it's this combination of hammer and
hand that is the tool.

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:15.190
And that's the object
that we can understand.

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:18.850
With digital objects, it's much more
complex to understand what were the things

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:20.840
people were using, touching.

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:22.190
How were they working?

00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:27.550
But if you think about the simplicity
of swiping your thumb left and

00:34:27.550 --> 00:34:29.590
right in an app for selection,

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:33.420
you start to understand how
critical objects interactions are.

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:34.550
And then other users.

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:39.515
Who else was there, what were people
doing, how were those people involved in

00:34:39.515 --> 00:34:43.780
either making the observable
behavior easier or harder to do?

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:45.237
And we find it, so

00:34:45.237 --> 00:34:50.400
a lot of times just looking at
a video of a user doing something.

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:51.370
Even just two or

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:56.650
three minutes of that video can take
several hours to decode what was going on.

00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:01.980
Looking at activities, environments,
interactions, objects, and users.

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:05.660
And then, to take that and
put it into an empathy framework.

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:06.770
This is what they're doing.

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:10.790
These are all the things we observed
them doing in all these different modes.

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:15.060
And then, those translates to
a mental model of what we think they,

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:19.150
what's happening in their brain, how were
they imaging the interaction to occur?

00:35:19.150 --> 00:35:22.300
Why were they getting stuck at
certain points and not others?

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:26.430
It's a really simple but very powerful
way of breaking down your observations.

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:31.710
And we really do encourage
video as a way of capturing,

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:36.670
not only the simple and observable stuff,
the body language, emotional state.

00:35:36.670 --> 00:35:40.050
When someone's frowning when
they're trying to do something or

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:42.920
smiling when they're trying to do
somethings tells you a lot about what's

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:46.250
the cognitive load that's
going on in their brain.

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:50.300
So, we get to that point and

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:54.550
then I want to talk about how do
you make this stuff actionable?

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:57.070
And I've alluded to it
about ten times now.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:02.490
You have to start with the assumption
that the information you can get

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:04.379
about the future is very limited.

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:09.426
You don't live in the future,
so not much you can do.

00:36:09.426 --> 00:36:12.583
And this was compounded
by the expert problem and

00:36:12.583 --> 00:36:15.590
by the problem of users
changing their needs.

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:22.564
So as you think about this, you, let's say
you've come back with observations and

00:36:22.564 --> 00:36:26.134
lot's of,
you've seen people using products and

00:36:26.134 --> 00:36:31.810
or services that are the things
you're interested in redesigning.

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:34.100
So you do have a pretty strong data set.

00:36:34.100 --> 00:36:39.670
You have a very nuanced set of
data about what's going on, what's

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:44.600
the cognitive model, where are the points
of friction, where is the cognitive load.

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:46.640
It may be higher than it should be.

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:48.070
Where are the points of delight?

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:52.807
Oftentimes, you have to do,
if you want to unseat an incumbent and

00:36:52.807 --> 00:36:57.978
they're doing a great job and
they're making something easy, Lyft,

00:36:57.978 --> 00:37:02.805
Uber, Tindr, Snapchat,
these apps that make instantaneous and

00:37:02.805 --> 00:37:06.730
ephemeral communication super easy to do.

00:37:06.730 --> 00:37:10.980
If you want to unseat them,
out-innovate them,

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:13.140
you're going to have to
be even more delightful.

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:16.200
It's not simply enough to
remove pain points, or

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:20.273
to remove cognitive discontinuity.

00:37:20.273 --> 00:37:23.864
You're going to have to out-delight them.

00:37:23.864 --> 00:37:28.621
So you've got all this information,
and then the only way we

00:37:28.621 --> 00:37:33.570
the actionable is this process
of prototyping and iteration.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:35.030
Prototyping and iteration.

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:40.070
So, I don't have a specific
example to show you, but

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:44.930
in the case of going all the way back to
my example of, all right, I'm working for

00:37:44.930 --> 00:37:49.100
a company that makes wheelbarrows and
we want to be the most innovative company.

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:52.094
And let's say we want to
grow ten times our size.

00:37:52.094 --> 00:37:54.490
Well, we're not going to find,

00:37:54.490 --> 00:37:59.470
I guess we could start selling
the wheelbarrow we've got in new markets.

00:37:59.470 --> 00:38:02.138
We could go to China, we could go to
India, we could go to other places.

00:38:02.138 --> 00:38:04.450
But that's a marketing solution,
and that's not.

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:08.370
That may be an innovation, but it's not
the same thing as a product innovation.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:12.740
So if we really wanted to say how do
you take a wheelbarrow company and

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:17.430
reframe it into a materials
logistics company, or

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:24.450
a getting stuff moved around where
you need it when you need it company.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:28.420
Simple prototypes like delivery.

00:38:28.420 --> 00:38:36.460
We could mock mock that up with a $100
drone from the toy store and paper bricks.

00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:41.610
And we could fly around job sites and
see what people thought about it.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.440
We could look sort of,
what's really is going on,

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:45.950
its an information's system problem.

00:38:45.950 --> 00:38:50.130
That stuff that we need is being
delivered to point to different

00:38:50.130 --> 00:38:51.600
places than where we need it.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:55.190
So, could we design an information
system that solves that problem?

00:38:55.190 --> 00:39:02.170
Could we design micro trucks that could
deliver stuff on site and almost directly.

00:39:02.170 --> 00:39:07.360
Which is kind of like what a forklift is,
only maybe an autonomous forklift

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:13.370
that the bricklayer could call from his
smart phone, like he calls an Uber.

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:17.560
So, you've got this, you create
a how might we statement which is

00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:21.940
based on your insight, but it's not about
wheelbarrows, it's about logistics.

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:29.670
How might we automate the delivery of
materials directly where they're needed?

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:33.360
Or how might we provide
an information system so

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:40.020
that logistics on the job
site are massively improved?

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:46.105
Or how might we create on-call systems so
that carpenters and

00:39:46.105 --> 00:39:52.903
brick layers can summon materials
where they want, when they want?

00:39:52.903 --> 00:39:54.810
So, each of those how might we statements

00:39:55.894 --> 00:39:59.047
Provokes a whole series
of possible prototypes.

00:39:59.047 --> 00:40:01.837
Because you don't know what's
the ideas and good ideas.

00:40:01.837 --> 00:40:03.661
But it's got an interesting resonance.

00:40:03.661 --> 00:40:08.533
It seems to match up some of the data
that you can match from the field with.

00:40:08.533 --> 00:40:11.648
I know it's a good how my reinstatement
when I can take a three prototypes,

00:40:11.648 --> 00:40:12.980
I want to go right way.

00:40:12.980 --> 00:40:17.611
And the prototypes are fast that
are inexpensive among other solution, and

00:40:17.611 --> 00:40:18.996
I learned something.

00:40:18.996 --> 00:40:22.523
All right, literally a $100
drone in paper bricks, and

00:40:22.523 --> 00:40:24.807
I can understand the psychological and

00:40:24.807 --> 00:40:30.080
cultural advantage and disadvantages of
flying stuff around the jobs sites can be.

00:40:30.080 --> 00:40:34.358
Maybe people will think it's so
cool, maybe they will think it's so

00:40:34.358 --> 00:40:38.064
dangerous and so out of control
that they would never do it.

00:40:38.064 --> 00:40:43.563
Or maybe carpenters and brick layers
are such conservative users that

00:40:43.563 --> 00:40:50.380
this kind of technology intervention they
would consider way, way, way too abrupt.

00:40:51.590 --> 00:40:55.750
I often think about the introduction of
Google Glass as an example of technology.

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:57.127
It's actually pretty cool.

00:40:57.127 --> 00:41:02.797
It was introduced and handled so poorly
that it became an instantaneous disaster,

00:41:02.797 --> 00:41:06.040
and that was even amongst
tech early adopters.

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:12.830
So you make things actionable
by building in the world.

00:41:12.830 --> 00:41:13.431
&gt;&gt; Thank you Bill.

00:41:13.431 --> 00:41:16.004
So thank you so much for
this wonderful presentation.

00:41:16.004 --> 00:41:21.387
It's always interesting to hear about all
the different ways that we can learn and

00:41:21.387 --> 00:41:25.922
engage with our customers and
arrive at so many unexpected results.

00:41:25.922 --> 00:41:29.185
So Bill one of the questions
that often comes up is

00:41:29.185 --> 00:41:32.790
around this question of failure and
risk taking.

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:35.820
What is the role of risk
taking in generating ideas?

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:41.163
How important is that and how do
people overcome that fear of failure?

00:41:41.163 --> 00:41:44.718
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a great question and
it comes up all the time.

00:41:44.718 --> 00:41:47.676
When we do the master class,
I often poll people in advance,

00:41:47.676 --> 00:41:50.570
about what do you want to
learn before you get there?

00:41:50.570 --> 00:41:52.261
And one of the things
they talk about is hey,

00:41:52.261 --> 00:41:54.333
my organization doesn't like
to take a lot of risks.

00:41:54.333 --> 00:41:59.301
We're a very conservative organization,
failure as a high really on cost around

00:41:59.301 --> 00:42:02.043
here, and yet
we still want to be innovative.

00:42:02.043 --> 00:42:02.995
What do we do?

00:42:02.995 --> 00:42:08.363
And I do a lot of tours of the school
as well, I'm talking to companies,

00:42:08.363 --> 00:42:11.983
and of course they say,
we want to be more innovative.

00:42:11.983 --> 00:42:15.265
And I go great,
what are you willing to change?

00:42:15.265 --> 00:42:16.922
And they say, nothing.

00:42:16.922 --> 00:42:18.240
And I go,
well that's going to be difficult.

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:23.158
[LAUGH] If you're trying to invent
something that's never been done before,

00:42:23.158 --> 00:42:27.849
if you're trying to create a product or
service or innovate in an area like

00:42:27.849 --> 00:42:32.860
the Wheelbarrow Company, then do
something that's never been done before.

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:40.162
Look at the venture capitalist, who were
supposed to be really smart invest in ten

00:42:40.162 --> 00:42:42.690
companies and then nine of them succeed.

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:46.530
70% of your product institutions
in the United States,

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:51.674
now by some measures failed to ever return
in 70% do not return the investment

00:42:51.674 --> 00:42:56.536
made in the development of those products
over the next two or three years.

00:42:56.536 --> 00:43:02.563
So 70% of products are failing and 90% of
venture capital companies are failing.

00:43:02.563 --> 00:43:07.230
And yet the ones that succeeded are so
powerful the Googles, the Apples,

00:43:07.230 --> 00:43:09.346
the Snapchats, the [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:09.346 --> 00:43:13.724
They're so powerful there
must be something about being

00:43:13.724 --> 00:43:17.104
comfortable with some level of of failure.

00:43:17.104 --> 00:43:21.833
Now, our idea is that you fail really
early in the process because that's when

00:43:21.833 --> 00:43:23.751
you can do the most learning.

00:43:23.751 --> 00:43:30.629
But if you have an organization that's
not willing to take risks at all,

00:43:30.629 --> 00:43:37.759
then my only question is if I believe
that we don't actually have a choice.

00:43:37.759 --> 00:43:42.732
That innovation is going to happen in your
marketplace whether you participate in

00:43:42.732 --> 00:43:43.258
or not.

00:43:43.258 --> 00:43:47.086
So your only choice is do I want to
participate in innovation and

00:43:47.086 --> 00:43:49.379
try to stay ahead of my competitors?

00:43:49.379 --> 00:43:53.663
Or am I willing to sort of
stay in a defensive posture,

00:43:53.663 --> 00:43:59.303
defend my position, continue to
extend my products incrementally?

00:43:59.303 --> 00:44:05.203
But boy if you Google or somebody
else comes around out innovates me,

00:44:05.203 --> 00:44:07.579
I will be caught back flooded.

00:44:07.579 --> 00:44:11.435
And probably see my markets and
my margins decline.

00:44:11.435 --> 00:44:15.420
So when these companies come, I say,
well you don't really have a choice.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:20.557
You either do the innovation or one of my
25 or 26 year old students going to go

00:44:20.557 --> 00:44:25.551
out and start a start up in Silicon
Valley, out innovate you in your space.

00:44:25.551 --> 00:44:30.078
And then you're going to be
faced with a declining market.

00:44:30.078 --> 00:44:35.088
So the correlation between
the ability to take and

00:44:35.088 --> 00:44:38.191
manage risk appropriately and

00:44:38.191 --> 00:44:42.854
to actually reward proper
kinds of failures.

00:44:42.854 --> 00:44:44.980
Failures that are learning opportunities.

00:44:44.980 --> 00:44:47.312
When we see companies doing those things,

00:44:47.312 --> 00:44:49.851
they tend to out innovate
their competitors.

00:44:49.851 --> 00:44:54.475
So it's just something you're going to
have to get used to managing like you

00:44:54.475 --> 00:44:57.518
manage any other risk in an organization.

00:44:57.518 --> 00:45:03.275
And look, 90% of your portfolio of
development activities will probably be,

00:45:03.275 --> 00:45:05.780
what we would call it
Apple line extensions.

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:11.660
There's simply the next Notebook,
the next iPad, the next phone.

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:15.579
But every once in a while, you're going to
be called upon, because the market

00:45:15.579 --> 00:45:19.519
forces to come up with a completely new,
completely not in a straight line.

00:45:19.519 --> 00:45:22.754
This is something knowing what's
predicting kind of product and

00:45:22.754 --> 00:45:25.465
in order to do it,
we have to be willing to fail.

00:45:25.465 --> 00:45:29.307
Remember the iPhone changed
failed three times with Steve.

00:45:29.307 --> 00:45:33.508
Turn down the iPhone three times and
say it wasn't innovative enough,

00:45:33.508 --> 00:45:35.582
wasn't good enough.

00:45:35.582 --> 00:45:38.270
And so if they can do that,
anybody can handle it,

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:41.662
because failing in front of Steve
was not applied from experience.

00:45:44.357 --> 00:45:45.557
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:45:45.557 --> 00:45:46.196
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and

00:45:46.196 --> 00:45:50.394
I think it's important to remember
that big companies can also fail.

00:45:50.394 --> 00:45:53.975
In fact I had a question from
one of the participants about,

00:45:53.975 --> 00:45:58.881
if a company such as Google can fail with
Google Glass, what will the rest of us,

00:45:58.881 --> 00:46:00.948
smaller and lots of us people do?

00:46:00.948 --> 00:46:04.912
And I think what you might be saying is
that this failure is part of the process.

00:46:04.912 --> 00:46:09.883
And you need to learn to live with it and
learn from it and benefit from it,

00:46:09.883 --> 00:46:11.115
is that correct?

00:46:11.115 --> 00:46:13.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and
just a quick comment on Google Glass.

00:46:13.770 --> 00:46:18.355
It's a classic example of actually
very poorly managed project and

00:46:18.355 --> 00:46:20.454
a very poorly executed thing.

00:46:20.454 --> 00:46:25.407
I don't think it represents a great
innovation on Google's part at all.

00:46:25.407 --> 00:46:30.179
It falls into the category of,
we see lots of start ups at Stanford and

00:46:30.179 --> 00:46:33.161
I saw some people come looking for advice.

00:46:33.161 --> 00:46:37.192
And they come in and they're so
excited about their product and

00:46:37.192 --> 00:46:42.146
they show it to you and it's so hard,
it's so technical, it's so amazing.

00:46:42.146 --> 00:46:48.941
You solved this huge technical problem and
then my said, great who needs it?

00:46:48.941 --> 00:46:49.829
What's this for?

00:46:49.829 --> 00:46:50.965
What problem does it solve?

00:46:50.965 --> 00:46:52.125
I said no, no, you don't get it.

00:46:52.125 --> 00:46:55.340
It's the technology, it's so cool.

00:46:55.340 --> 00:46:58.000
The problem with Google Glasses
was all technology.

00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:00.790
They didn't take into
account the social and

00:47:00.790 --> 00:47:03.480
emotional issues of wearing
something on your face.

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:08.428
They didn't take into account the privacy
issues of having an always on camera that

00:47:08.428 --> 00:47:12.388
didn't give any signal to the non
participant that they were being

00:47:12.388 --> 00:47:13.393
photographed.

00:47:13.393 --> 00:47:20.649
And so it violated, so many social and
sort of privacy norms.

00:47:20.649 --> 00:47:24.884
And it included the face in the way
that made the person look foolish.

00:47:24.884 --> 00:47:28.548
And so it was like all of these things
were easily discoverable had they done

00:47:28.548 --> 00:47:29.433
some refunding.

00:47:29.433 --> 00:47:33.772
But it's what we call a technology push
project, where they are just pushing

00:47:33.772 --> 00:47:37.393
technology into the world and
hoping to find a quote market fit.

00:47:37.393 --> 00:47:39.136
That almost never works.

00:47:42.145 --> 00:47:45.754
&gt;&gt; Interesting, that's helpful.

00:47:45.754 --> 00:47:47.888
Are there any specific traits or

00:47:47.888 --> 00:47:51.682
characteristics of people that
you look for that could be

00:47:51.682 --> 00:47:56.203
more successful in performing
design thinking activities, etc?

00:47:56.203 --> 00:48:00.830
Is there something that you,
Recommend looking for or

00:48:00.830 --> 00:48:05.760
kind of developing as a person
wanting to do more of that.

00:48:05.760 --> 00:48:07.880
&gt;&gt; There is actually and it's interesting.

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:11.100
If you're an undergraduate in my
program or a graduate student,

00:48:11.100 --> 00:48:15.420
we'll teach you the entire process
from empathy to prototyping,

00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:19.480
through manufacturing, production,
business planning and stuff.

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:22.590
But of course, some people are really
good at one or two of the things.

00:48:22.590 --> 00:48:24.260
Some people are really
good at business planning.

00:48:24.260 --> 00:48:27.360
Some people are really good
at understanding markets and

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:30.650
how to distribute- distribute
things very cleverly.

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:33.350
But there's a class of people
that are just really good

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:36.790
at the design research part,
the empathy observation part.

00:48:36.790 --> 00:48:40.260
They tend to have very high social IQ, so

00:48:40.260 --> 00:48:43.800
their social emotional
intelligence is very high.

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:46.810
They tend to be incredibly good listeners,

00:48:46.810 --> 00:48:49.800
because mostly this is just
about listening and observing.

00:48:49.800 --> 00:48:51.660
It's not so much about interviewing.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:56.045
It's interesting,
people who are great news reporters,

00:48:56.045 --> 00:49:01.690
like a friend of mine teaches journalism
at the Emerson College in Boston.

00:49:01.690 --> 00:49:03.700
And he was a journalist for
many years for the Boston Globe.

00:49:03.700 --> 00:49:06.650
And he says,
good journalists are just nosy people.

00:49:06.650 --> 00:49:07.470
They're not good listeners.

00:49:07.470 --> 00:49:11.535
They just want to pry into other people's
lives and find out what's going one and

00:49:11.535 --> 00:49:12.210
write it up.

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:15.298
That's the opposite of
an empathetic listener.

00:49:15.298 --> 00:49:20.108
So I've noticed some of my students just
have a natural tendency towards listening.

00:49:20.108 --> 00:49:26.554
They have an open sort of gregariousness,
which invites people to talk to them.

00:49:26.554 --> 00:49:30.430
They're the kind of folks that give, they
were sitting on an airplane, they would

00:49:30.430 --> 00:49:35.800
talk to everybody in their section at
some point, but they're not overbearing.

00:49:35.800 --> 00:49:41.520
So the high social emotional intelligence
are sort of probably an extrovert or

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:46.880
open mindset tend to be the best
at the design research side.

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:50.945
Now that's all learnable, for sure, but
some people just have it naturally.

00:49:50.945 --> 00:49:55.700
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:49:55.700 --> 00:49:58.282
So I think we have just three
more minutes, we'll have time for

00:49:58.282 --> 00:49:59.119
one more question.

00:49:59.119 --> 00:50:02.741
Before we take that, just a reminder
to everybody that we will be,

00:50:02.741 --> 00:50:04.553
we are recording this webinar and

00:50:04.553 --> 00:50:08.194
then email with the link to the recording
will be send to all of you.

00:50:08.194 --> 00:50:12.918
So last question, you described earlier
that it might take a few hours to

00:50:12.918 --> 00:50:18.120
kind of get all the data and information
from even a few minutes of video.

00:50:18.120 --> 00:50:22.900
Could that lead to over analysis of
the data that you're collecting?

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:23.780
And how do you avoid that?

00:50:25.180 --> 00:50:27.980
&gt;&gt; Well, one of the mindsets I
mentioned in, I think the third slide,

00:50:27.980 --> 00:50:29.540
was a bias tax.

00:50:29.540 --> 00:50:32.650
So although you do wrap the field,
you collect all this video and data.

00:50:32.650 --> 00:50:36.340
And, by the way, someone mentioned,
does everybody go on the team?

00:50:36.340 --> 00:50:39.347
We typically go in twos,
because someone can do the interview,

00:50:39.347 --> 00:50:42.747
while someone's taking notes or
running the cameras very discreetly,

00:50:42.747 --> 00:50:46.331
because you'd like the person talking
to not want to have to be taking notes.

00:50:46.331 --> 00:50:50.472
But when you come back and two, three or
four teams have all talked to different

00:50:50.472 --> 00:50:53.548
users, you some need time to
collate that information and

00:50:53.548 --> 00:50:56.150
just sort of chalk through it and
understand it.

00:50:56.150 --> 00:51:00.674
But as soon as you believe you have
even a cursory understanding or

00:51:00.674 --> 00:51:05.034
beginning understanding of the users,
form a restatement and

00:51:05.034 --> 00:51:10.740
build a prototype, because your
dataset's going to be incomplete anyway.

00:51:10.740 --> 00:51:13.300
And you know that because as
soon as you build a prototype,

00:51:13.300 --> 00:51:18.010
the users will, I say change their needs,
but what they really will do is they'll

00:51:18.010 --> 00:51:22.170
just evoke new responses now that they
know there are other possibilities.

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:28.490
So the biased action would say
spend 70% of your time in the field

00:51:28.490 --> 00:51:32.620
either interviewing or trying prototypes
and 30% of your time on the analysis.

00:51:32.620 --> 00:51:37.790
And we actually measure our student team,
or masters teams, they have to tell me

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:41.773
every week how much time they spent doing
and how much time they spent talking.

00:51:41.773 --> 00:51:44.200
[COUGH] And if I don't see a 70 30 split,

00:51:44.200 --> 00:51:48.140
I push them back out into their
field to do more field work.

00:51:48.140 --> 00:51:53.280
The prototype building and
testing is part of the empathy process.

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:57.840
It's where the back part of the process
connects back again to the front part.

00:52:00.035 --> 00:52:02.090
Anyway, I think we're
probably out of time.

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:03.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Thanks everybody for listening, and

00:52:03.750 --> 00:52:05.870
it appears everybody hung in there for
the whole time.

00:52:05.870 --> 00:52:06.650
We really appreciate it.

00:52:08.460 --> 00:52:09.660
&gt;&gt; Thank you everyone.

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:11.162
Have a good rest of your day.

00:52:11.162 --> 00:52:12.964
Thank you for
spending your morning with us.

